    <DataNode elementId="AT2G23000-MONOMER" textLabel="Serine carboxypeptidase-like 10" type="Protein">
      <Xref identifier="O64810" dataSource="uniprot" />
      <Graphics centerX="300" centerY="635" width="90.0" height="25.0" textColor="000000" fontName="Arial" fontWeight="Normal" fontStyle="Normal" fontDecoration="Normal" fontStrikethru="Normal" fontSize="12" hAlign="Center" vAlign="Middle" borderColor="000000" borderStyle="Solid" borderWidth="1.0" fillColor="FFFFFF" shapeType="Rectangle" />
      <Comment source="pmngroup">Five serine carboxypeptidase-like (SCPL) genes are arranged in tandem on chromosome 2 of |FRAME: TAX-3702 &lt;i&gt;Arabidopsis thaliana&lt;/i&gt;|. The genes are highly similar, with any two of them being between 71% and 78% identical |CITS: [15908604]|. One of these genes (|FRAME: AT2G22990 SNG1|) was known to encode a |FRAME: AT2G22990-MONOMER sinapoylglucose:malate &lt;i&gt;O&lt;/i&gt;-sinapoyltransferase| |CITS: [10948250]|. A study of the other genes, utilizing several fast neutron-generated mutant plants, revealed that three of these genes (|FRAME: AT2G23000 AT2G23000|, |FRAME: AT2G23010 AT2G23010| and |FRAME: AT2G22980 AT2G22980|) also encode sinapoyl-glucose-dependent acyltransferases |CITS: [17600138]|. A mutant named sng1-5 was shown to be affected in |FRAME: AT2G22990 SNG1| and the gene immediately downstream (|FRAME: AT2G23000 AT2G23000|). When grown under conditions that induce anthocyanin accumulation, the mutant failed to produce any of the sinapoylated anthocyanins that are usually produced, including |FRAME: CPD-16540 cyanidin 3-&lt;i&gt;O&lt;/i&gt;-[6-&lt;i&gt;O&lt;/i&gt;-(4-&lt;i&gt;O&lt;/i&gt;-&amp;beta;-D-glucosyl-&lt;i&gt;p&lt;/i&gt;-coumaroyl)-2-&lt;i&gt;O&lt;/i&gt;-(2-&lt;i&gt;O&lt;/i&gt;-sinapoyl-&amp;beta;-D-xylosyl)-&amp;beta;-D-glucosyl]-5-&lt;i&gt;O&lt;/i&gt;-(6-&lt;i&gt;O&lt;/i&gt;-malonyl-&amp;beta;-D-glucoside)|, the major anthocyanin in Arabidopsis |CITS: [11830144]|. Another mutant, named sng1-1, which was only defficient in |FRAME: AT2G22990 SNG1|, did produce these compounds. Since the only difference between the two mutants was the absence of |FRAME: AT2G23000 AT2G23000| in sng1-5, these data suggest that |FRAME: AT2G23000 AT2G23000| encodes a |FRAME: AT2G23000-MONOMER sinapoyl-&amp;beta;-D-glucose:anthocyanin sinapoyltransferase|, an enzyme that is required for the synthesis of the sinapoylated anthocyanins in Arabidopsis |CITS: [17600138]|. The |FRAME: AT2G23000 AT2G23000| gene was already suspected to have a role in anthocyanin biosynthesis since it is expressed in senescing leaves |CITS: [15908604]|, a tissue that is frequently observed to accumulate anthocyanin pigments, and since it was shown to be up-regulated in the pap1-D (production of anthocyanin pigment 1-Dominant) mutant, which accumulates anthocyanins to abnormally high levels |CITS: [15807784]|. In a later study the various sgn1 mutants were stressed with high sucrose to induce anthocyanin biosynthesis. The sgn1-5 mutant was the only one that showed no accumulation of sinapoylated anthocyanins, indicating that At2g23000 indeed encodes the sinapoyl glucose: anthocyanin acyltransferase (SAT) responsible for the formation of |FRAME: CPD-16539 cyanidin 3-&lt;i&gt;O&lt;/i&gt;-[2&quot;-&lt;i&gt;O&lt;/i&gt;-(2&quot;&apos;-&lt;i&gt;O&lt;/i&gt;-(sinapoyl) xylosyl) 6&quot;-&lt;i&gt;O&lt;/i&gt;-(p-coumaroyl) glucoside] 5-&lt;i&gt;O&lt;/i&gt;-[6&quot;&quot;-&lt;i&gt;O&lt;/i&gt;-(malonyl) glucoside| |CITS: [17600138]|.</Comment>
      <Property key="UniqueID" value="AT2G23000-MONOMER" />
